 $y$ can be written as \n$y=\\frac{{D}_{y}}{|\\begin{aligned}-6 & 8 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{aligned}|},$ \nwhere ${D}_{y}=$" 13194139534854 17592186045417 true] [17592186120447 1071 "Consider the system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-6x+8y=-46 \\\\ 6x+5y=20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the unknown $y$ can be written as \n$y=\\frac{{D}_{y}}{|\\begin{aligned}-6 & 8 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{aligned}|},$ \nwhere ${D}_{y}=$" 13194139534854 17592186045417 false] [17592186120447 1074 "We are given the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-6x+8y=-46 \\\\ 6x+5y=20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}-6 & \\,8 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to construct ${D}_{y}$, we replace the second column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y} & =|\\begin{aligned}-6 & -46 \\\\ 6 & 20\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534854 17592186045417 true] [17592186120447 1074 "We are given the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-6x+8y=-46 \\\\ 6x+5y=20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}-6 & \\,8 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to construct ${D}_{y}$, we replace the second column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y} & =|\\begin{aligned}-6 & -46 \\\\ 6 & 20\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534854 17592186045417 false] [17592186120448 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534854 17592186045417 true] [17592186120448 1071 "Let $⟨x, 3⟩$ be the solution of the system of equations below.\n${2x + 3y = 10; kx - 4y = 12$.\nWhich of the following equations could be solved to find the value of $k$?\n\n1. $3\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ k & -4|=\\end{vmatrix}2\\begin{vmatrix}10 \\\\ k\\end{vmatrix}12 \\mid$\n2. $\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ k & -4|=\\end{vmatrix}2\\begin{vmatrix}10 \\\\ k\\end{vmatrix}12 \\mid$\n3. $\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ k & -4|= 3\\end{vmatrix}2\\begin{vmatrix}10 \\\\ k\\end{vmatrix}12 \\mid$" 13194139534854 17592186045417 false] [17592186120448 1071 "Let $⟨x,3⟩$ be the solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+3y=10 \\\\ kx-4y=12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following equations could be solved to find the value of $k?$\n\nI. $3|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ k & -4\\end{aligned}|=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 10 \\\\ k & 12\\end{aligned}|$\n\nII. $|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ k & -4\\end{aligned}|=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 10 \\\\ k & 12\\end{aligned}|$\n\nIII. $|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ k & -4\\end{aligned}|=3|\\begin{aligned}2 & 10 \\\\ k & 12\\end{aligned}|$" 13194139534854 17592186045417 true] [17592186120448 1074 "The determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ k & -4\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $y$ is given by $y=\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y} & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 10 \\\\ k & 12\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D} \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}2 & 10 \\\\ k & 12\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ k & -4\\end{aligned}|} \\\\ 3|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ k & -4\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 10 \\\\ k & 12\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139534854 17592186045417 true] [17592186121027 1064 "**Cramer's Rule** is a method for solving a linear system of equations using determinants.\n\nSuppose we have a square system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ![](images/c7/c7ef883a1d4dd8c1fc0fc8fbde5ed80c9a5229650fc77c77559b71552370c35b.svg) Then Cramer's rule states that\n\n$$\nx =  \\dfrac{D_x}{D}, \\qquad y = \\dfrac{D_y}{D}\n$$\n\nis the unique solution of the system, where\n![](images/b1/b13ecd10395fe0977d04d627179667cbca207fcf4015a89d43df0e69765f1f5e.svg)\n\nFor example, let's use Cramer's rule to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3x-2y=-1 \\\\ -1x+2y=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by finding the **determinant of the coefficient matrix**:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $D_x$ and $D_y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x} & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 2-3\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =4 \\\\ {D}_{y} & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D}=\\frac{4}{4}=1, \\\\ y & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D}=\\frac{8}{4}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt is common to write the solutions to a linear system as a vector. So, in this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\left< x,y \\right> = \\left< 1,2 \\right>.\n$$" 13194139534854 17592186045417 true] [17592186121027 1064 "**Cramer's Rule** is a method for solving a linear system of equations using determinants.\n\nSuppose we have a square system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $|\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}|$ Then Cramer's rule states that\n\n$$\nx =  \\dfrac{D_x}{D}, \\qquad y = \\dfrac{D_y}{D}\n$$\n\nis the unique solution of the system, where\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {b}_{2} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nFor example, let's use Cramer's rule to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3x-2y=-1 \\\\ -1x+2y=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by finding the **determinant of the coefficient matrix**:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $D_x$ and $D_y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x} & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 2-3\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =4 \\\\ {D}_{y} & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D}=\\frac{4}{4}=1, \\\\ y & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D}=\\frac{8}{4}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt is common to write the solutions to a linear system as a vector. So, in this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\left< x,y \\right> = \\left< 1,2 \\right>.\n$$" 13194139534854 17592186045417 false] [17592186271976 1071 "Consider the system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-3x+11y=-23 \\\\ 5x-y=21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the unknown $y$ can be written as \n$y=\\frac{{D}_{y}}{|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 11 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}|},$ \nwhere ${D}_{y}=$" 13194139534854 17592186045417 true] [17592186271976 1071 "Consider the system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3x+11y=-23 \\\\ 5x-y=21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the unknown $y$ can be written as \n$y=\\frac{{D}_{y}}{|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 11 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}|},$ \nwhere ${D}_{y}=$" 13194139534854 17592186045417 false] [17592186271976 1074 "We are given the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-3x+11y=-23 \\\\ 5x-y=21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}-3 & \\,11 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to construct ${D}_{y}$, we replace the second column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y} & =|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -23 \\\\ 5 & 21\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534854 17592186045417 true]